WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.984
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.984 --> 00:00:05.400
SARAH GALLOP: Commencement--
one of the most exciting days

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.455
of the year.

00:00:06.455 --> 00:00:07.830
JOHN MCDONALD:
It's not too often

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:12.015
you have a family member or a
loved one graduating from MIT.

00:00:12.015 --> 00:00:14.390
ERIC GRIMSON: It's like inviting
somebody into your home.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:17.640
You want it to look good.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.170
PING LEE: When the rhododendrons
are blooming, it's beautiful.

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:23.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:24.488
[HAMMERING]

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.330
MATT MCGANN: It's really
logistically challenging.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.620
TED JOHNSON: We, last
year, had 2856 graduates,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.860
and we presented 2300
pounds of diplomas.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.360
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.140
ERIC GRIMSON: Students
get their diploma,

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.200
their piece of history, from
the President or the Provost

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.430
on stage.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:55.172
To make that happen is
an incredible amount

00:00:55.172 --> 00:00:56.255
of work behind the scenes.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:03.970
Our registrar staff is
preparing 2500-plus diplomas

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:08.500
to be in exactly
the right order.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.510
MARY CALLAHAN: We're
very careful and precise,

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:14.530
and it's a team effort
by a group of my staff.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:15.690
There is a great flux.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.600
Students are hoping
to make their diploma.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.740
Some do, some don't.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.517
So we're continually
monitoring advising students.

00:01:23.517 --> 00:01:25.100
ELIZABETH COGLIANO
YOUNG: They line up

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.710
according to their degree and
school in alphabetical order.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.840
There are 70 station
aides, and then there's

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.940
a team of marshals
that then will

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.920
lead each of these specific
groups out to Killian Court.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.808
PRESENTER: Please close
any gaps in the line.

00:01:40.808 --> 00:01:42.928
Stay close to the
person in front of you.

00:01:45.856 --> 00:01:46.730
PRESENTER: Last name?

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:47.270
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:48.100
PRESENTER: Thank you.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:48.510
AUDIENCE: Li.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:48.990
PRESENTER: Jessica?

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:49.425
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:01:49.425 --> 00:01:49.700
PRESENTER: Thank you.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:50.310
PRESENTER: Last name?

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:50.400
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.886
PRESENTER: Thank you, thank you.

00:01:51.886 --> 00:01:52.760
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:53.830
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:54.750
PRESENTER: Uh-huh.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.750
MARY CALLAHAN: They have
to say their last name,

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.250
and we'll be checking
at that point

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.920
to make sure the student
hasn't dropped inadvertently

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.050
out of line or got
lost or something.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.610
PRESENTER: He is absent
but was marked here.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:06.720
PRESENTER: Yes.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:07.460
PRESENTER: OK.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.440
MARY CALLAHAN: My office
keeps the master list

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.520
of the marching order.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.735
PRESENTER: Right, thank you.

00:02:11.735 --> 00:02:12.050
AUDIENCE: Wu.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.025
PRESENTER: Thank you.

00:02:13.025 --> 00:02:13.969
Last name?

00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:14.510
AUDIENCE: Wu.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:17.090
PING LEE: There is checks
upon checks upon checks,

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:21.670
because one student gets out
of line, it's a domino effect.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.154
PRESENTER: Jamila S Smith-Dell.

00:02:23.154 --> 00:02:25.570
ELIZABETH COGLIANO YOUNG: As
a graduate crosses the stage,

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:27.482
they are handed
their actual diploma.

00:02:27.482 --> 00:02:28.940
Whereas many other
schools, they're

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.606
mailed their actual
diploma, and they're

00:02:30.606 --> 00:02:32.812
handed a fake certificate
at the time of graduation,

00:02:32.812 --> 00:02:34.520
we want the students
to actually come off

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:35.890
the stage holding
their actual degree.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:36.560
[CHEERING]

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.910
So it is a very
flexible, dynamic walking

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:44.460
of 2800 people to Killian Court.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.920
MARY CALLAHAN: I've
always felt that for MIT

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.040
to do this for their students
says a lot about MIT.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.680
SARAH GALLOP: Yu Hua.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.900
Elliot S Ishmael.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.840
Rachel M Hoffman.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.530
Athena Y Huang.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:02.370
Lakeisha Jane.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:06.720
Students will leave me the
pronunciation of their names

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.145
on my voicemail.

00:03:08.145 --> 00:03:11.590
MAXIMILIANO ZULOAGA (ON
PHONE): Maximiliano Zuloaga.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:15.590
SARAH GALLOP:
Maximiliano Zuloaga.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.170
Maximiliano Sebastian Zuloaga.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.690
MICHAEL OWU: The real trick is
getting where the emphasis is

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:22.650
in someone's name.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:27.400
SARAH GALLOP:
Day-miss W Luh-moge.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:27.900
Luh-moge.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.140
And if you place emphasis
on the wrong syll-a-bull,

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.420
then you can really
botch a name.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.900
Andrew J Unander.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.470
[? Jin-wen ?] Wang.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.050
MICHAEL OWU: It's not just the
complexity of an individual's

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:47.400
name, but it's going from a
Chinese name to an Indian name

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.050
to an American name
to a German name.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:52.020
It's that switching
from one language

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.700
to the other that can
sometimes be challenging.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.080
PRESENTER: Camille I Henrot.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.060
PRESENTER: Nigamaa Nayakanti.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.152
PRESENTER: Amy S Ishiguro.

00:04:01.152 --> 00:04:02.610
MICHAEL OWU: The
ones who know they

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:06.360
have a difficult name, when
you get it right, they smile.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:07.470
It's a good feeling.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.630
PRESENTER: Nithin Senthur Kumar.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.710
PRESENTER: Marie P [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.951
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.309
TED JOHNSON: One of the greatest
challenges is the scale.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.110
To set up 14,000 chairs
takes hundreds of people.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.740
NORM MAGNUSON: Basically
what you're doing

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:37.287
is taking Killian Court and
making it into a big stadium.

00:04:37.287 --> 00:04:40.696
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:51.761 --> 00:04:53.802
MATT MCGANN: We set up
these great big jumbotrons

00:04:53.802 --> 00:04:55.530
PRESENTER: I like to
see a lot of really

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.848
happy, waving, smiling,
happy families out there.

00:04:58.848 --> 00:05:01.770
PRESENTER: One, pan left,
and go back to the podium.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.775
[INAUDIBLE] one, take one.

00:05:03.775 --> 00:05:05.230
MATT MCGANN: For
tens of thousands

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.240
of people around
the world who want

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.872
to be a part of
this special day,

00:05:08.872 --> 00:05:10.330
they can watch this
on the webcast.

00:05:13.188 --> 00:05:15.188
PRESENTER: There's waters
and everything inside.

00:05:15.188 --> 00:05:16.634
Please have your tickets ready.

00:05:16.634 --> 00:05:17.610
Congratulations.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.020
JOHN MCDONALD: We have about
250 volunteers each year

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:22.020
that handle different
functions for our guests--

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:23.728
making sure that they
get into the Court,

00:05:23.728 --> 00:05:25.590
having a good place
to sit, having

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:26.760
some information available.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:27.704
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:05:27.704 --> 00:05:30.070
PRESENTER: Thank you so much.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:32.340
PING LEE: We are there to
take care of the guests--

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.720
make sure they know
where they're going,

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.050
they all have water,
they all have programs,

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.480
they all have a seat.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.850
TED JOHNSON: We
order bottled water,

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.024
probably nearly 20,000 bottles.

00:05:45.024 --> 00:05:47.892
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:50.170
This is New England.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:53.780
The weather can go
to the extremes.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:56.650
We have on hand 18,000 ponchos.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:58.840
They are available
to our graduates too,

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:02.940
and we encourage them to
wear under their gowns.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:05.190
NORM MAGNUSON: The people
that come from other jobs

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:08.700
because they volunteer to do it,
it's really important to them

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.260
that all the chairs
are straight,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:12.240
that everything looks beautiful.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.530
And I think that it gives them
a sense of serving the students,

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.445
and it makes them feel
good about their job.

00:06:18.445 --> 00:06:20.070
ELIZABETH COGLIANO
YOUNG: It gives time

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.920
to pause and sort of think, wow,
what an amazing place to work,

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:26.040
and how lucky I am to be part
of such a great experience.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:27.240
PRESENTER: Congratulations.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.040
[CHEERING]

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

